00:11:53.000 --> 00:12:11.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, so I think we'll go ahead and kick things off for today. Thank you so much, everybody, for being here. I think I know everybody in the room, but I'm Whitney, one of the co-founders of Wedding Industry Ads. I think Daniella is here with us today, too.

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:32.000
Whitney Hodges: But we're so pleased to be welcoming our friend, Emily Foster, who is an amazing brand strategist and website designer. We've collaborated on a fair amount of clients, I think, over the years together at this point. And so just to kind of give you guys some more context and some more background.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:27.000
Emily Foster: Wow.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:49.000
Whitney Hodges: I do a workshop twice a month for members of our ads accelerators programs where I go live on Tuesdays, share more insights, more curriculum, and then it gets added to our curriculum in the vault.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:13:07.000
Whitney Hodges: So, um, you know, I think we've got some members of our accelerator programs in the room here today. We've got some current clients, we've got some past clients. I really wanted to open today up to anybody that wanted to be here, just because the foundations for having success with your advertising and just your marketing in general.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:22.000
Whitney Hodges: are so important. Um, and that's really why I wanted to invite Emily to be here, because she really is such an expert on that. You know, any clients that we kind of work to work together on, um, everything just always feels so in alignment.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:37.000
Whitney Hodges: in alignment in terms of their websites, their branding, and we see those results in their ads results as well, right? Because the messaging really hits home with their potential clients. It builds that trust, um, along the way. So…

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:55.000
Whitney Hodges: that's why I invited Emily here today to talk to us about building a brand that supports premium lead generation. So, um, we're gonna let Emily kind of take it away from here. If you guys have questions that are coming up throughout the training, feel free to pop them into the chat. I'll kind of keep an eye on those.

00:13:55.000 --> 00:14:03.000
Whitney Hodges: And then at the end, we'll go ahead and open it up for Q&A. So, thanks again, Emily, so much for being here.

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:26.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, thank you so much for the really nice intro. It's been so awesome working with you all. And yeah, I love, we've had a lot of clients work with Whitney and Daniela after their rebrands and website designs, and it's really fun to see like the growth that happens. I kind of feel like your website, I have other metaphors in this presentation, ironically, but I feel like your website is kind of like the match and like the fire starter kit and.

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:43.000
Emily Foster: like, as that grows, like, SEO, it's the fire, and then it's, like, when you run ads, it's, like, pouring gasoline on the fire, and so it's, like, really fun to see the results that people get when they combine both of these marketing strategies. Um, so I'm really excited to dive into what I really think that you need to consider as the foundation.

00:14:43.000 --> 00:15:03.000
Emily Foster: Um, ideally before running ads, but if you are running ads right now, like, it's never too late to continue to work on updating your website, and that can actually be a really cool way to see, like, um, like, A-B tests, basically, how your ads have performed when you've, um, maybe not optimized your site fully, and your brand, and then after, and, like, see the difference between that.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:15.000
Emily Foster: Um, so we're going to talk about how your brand, website, and consistency work together to attract your ideal clients, and building that foundational kind of base for running ads, too.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:37.000
Emily Foster: Um, so I think, like, one of the best metaphors is honestly thinking about your website as a storefront, and, like, like, just your entire online presence, so even beyond your website, like, your social media profiles and the ads that you're running, just any way that you exist, because I know most of you, unless you have, like, a venue or, like, maybe a physical floral space or something like that, you're not typically.

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:55.000
Emily Foster: attracting people to a physical location. So, imagine that you're walking along the street, and you see a sign for a store that sounds so interesting, um, or maybe you've been scrolling on a meta platform or Google, and you see something that sounds, like, really interesting, um, and it sounds exactly like it's perfect for you.

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:57.000
Emily Foster: But then when you walk into the store.

00:15:57.000 --> 00:16:17.000
Emily Foster: it is a totally different vibe. Like, maybe it's, like, dark and dingy, and the outside was, like, bright and luxury, and felt like, okay, this is the place for me, but then when you walk in, you're like, oh, I don't think I belong here. I feel like it's pretty rare that we have this experience in person, but it's such a common thing that we experience online, where we're.

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:33.000
Emily Foster: kind of throwing money at ads, and, like, creating this really great content, but then when they get from the ad to the website, the vibes just, like, aren't different. So, you're not really sure if you ended up in the right place, and maybe you walk out, or in virtual sense, you click out.

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:50.000
Emily Foster: Or even worse, what I see in the wedding industry, I bet Whitney and Daniela see this a lot too, but it's like, they get to the website, and then it's practically empty. So it's like, imagine getting to that storefront, it's beautiful, and then you, like, walk in, and there's, like, not a selection of anything to look at.

00:16:50.000 --> 00:17:05.000
Emily Foster: Um, there's not really, like, anything there that's aligned with what you're looking for, and you kind of feel like you wasted your time. I see this a ton, whether people are running ads or not, um, even, like, when people have kind of, you know, gone through this website process, and then.

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:23.000
Emily Foster: they launch the website, and it's just, like, beautiful galleries, or some information that's, like, not really aligned with their clients, and I think it's hard for us to sometimes take, like, a, um, larger look at our own websites, because we get into the weeds of everything. You're, like, delivering for clients, and.

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:44.000
Emily Foster: You're curating your galleries and like trying to do all of the things right. And sometimes like trying to do all of those things makes it harder to see the overall strategy of how your website is actually serving you and speaking to your clients. So this is where a lot of people get into this pitfall of like thinking they have everything on their website or maybe they even have too much and it's not aligned with what their clients are looking for.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:18:03.000
Emily Foster: Um, so, ads are really the advertising for your store, that's why it's called… they're called ads and advertising, and then your modern store is your website. So, really, your ads should be attracting to your website, um, and bringing people in, and then your website should be doing the work of converting.

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:24.000
Emily Foster: and, like, converting people to inquiries. And it's essential that both of them work in harmony for your clients to feel intrigued and excited, and not kind of, like, um, baited and switched when they get to the website. That's where you're really going to see the best conversions, and I think that's why we see such great conversions with people who decide to partner with, like, both my team and then the Wedding Industry Ads team.

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:41.000
Emily Foster: Because they're doing this consistent work to make sure that everything down to the ads creative, and the keywords used in the ads, and then all of the language on the website, and all of the aesthetics on the website speak a similar language, and actually speak to the specific client that they're wanting to target.

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:57.000
Emily Foster: Um, so, leads through ads, I honestly think mean nothing if they're not quality leads. That's where we want, especially as a small business, we want every single penny to be working to attract people who will actually book with you. Um, and I say this a lot in, like, presentations, too, even when we're not talking about, like.

00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:14.000
Emily Foster: ads, but I think… I kind of have a hot take that I feel like, ideally, the only people that would get on sales calls with you are the people who are going to book with you. I know a lot of coaches are like, oh, you even need to look at, like, a conversion rate, but honestly, your time is so valuable as a business owner that my hope for you is that you have a good enough.

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:29.000
Emily Foster: selection process and kind of filtration process through your ads and through your website that by the time someone inquires with you, um, they, if they actually book a call, then they're aligned. And that also means your sales process too, like having some filtration there so that.

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:45.000
Emily Foster: If someone can't afford your services or they're not really a good personality fit, ideally they're just not taking the time to get on that call and your time on calls is being really well used to book people who are going to really add to your business and who you can really help and enjoy working with.

00:19:45.000 --> 00:20:04.000
Emily Foster: We ideally want them to be what I call future raving fans, so these are the people that are going to trust you, love working with you, and be excited to refer you after the process, because that referral strategy is also going to really help build your business beyond that. So it's really this entire funnel, of course, ads are the beginning, and.

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:20.000
Emily Foster: It matters so much to the process, and then we want to optimize the website and think about this long term of building that, like, raving fan base so that you have people who are passionate working with you, and you have that consistent lead generation through all of these kind of different buckets in your marketing strategy.

00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:39.000
Emily Foster: Um, and the thing I always come back to, even though we're, like, all small businesses, and we don't necessarily have the same goals, but there's this secret in… it's not really a secret, it's a secret not-so-secret, um, in the industry that billion-dollar companies have mastered that we sometimes fall short of.

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:55.000
Emily Foster: of as small businesses. And all of these companies have very successful ad campaigns and spend millions, like multimillion dollars on ads, including digital ad strategies. And the reason that they're so successful all comes down to their brand consistency and recognition.

00:20:55.000 --> 00:21:12.000
Emily Foster: It really is a difference in ad strategies that each of them plays, and they all like have different budgets, and they all like have different ways that they speak to their clients, but they're all consistent, and they build recognition with their campaigns, and that follows through to their websites.

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:30.000
Emily Foster: So you aren't going to see these businesses spending millions of dollars on ad campaigns just to send customers to an empty or unaligned site. Or if you think of a physical product, like any of those brands, like you're not going to see them not investing money in their packaging or their shelving or like their retail displays.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:47.000
Emily Foster: just to spend millions on commercials. It's kind of like the analogy. So, we really want you, like, if you are taking the time to spend hundreds or thousands of dollars on your ads, you want to be sure that you've done the foundational work to make it work by having an aligned site, and one that has the kind of content that your clients are looking for.

00:21:47.000 --> 00:22:09.000
Emily Foster: And fun fact, kind of scary, I think, but you get 0.05 seconds on your website to impress your audience. So that's the other thing is like, even if the ad is written so eloquently and the creative is perfect, when they land on your page, if they don't see what they like in less than a quarter of a second.

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:29.000
Emily Foster: they can click out. And no matter what kind of hard work, like, Whitney and Daniella's team does for you, it's really hard to get that traffic back. Like, you, of course, can have retargeting strategies and things like that, but it really is hard to build that trust right away if you don't have that great first impression on your website. So we'll be talking about that today, and.

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:33.000
Emily Foster: some things I want you to pay attention to when you're optimizing your site.

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:54.000
Emily Foster: Okay, while ads handle attraction and warming leads, a website gives you full control over the narrative as well. So I love that it really is the place in the internet that you own. It's the place that you get to control the most. You can be very intentional about your SEO strategy and the way that you're targeting people. You get to be very intentional about the messaging and the branding that's applied.

00:22:54.000 --> 00:23:08.000
Emily Foster: And so it really is your business's home base. It's like the retail store where you have full control over that inner design. Um, so we really want to make sure that every single part of the website is working for you and, um, aligning with your strategy.

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:26.000
Emily Foster: So just ask yourself as we go into this, what is your website doing to convert the valuable leads that ads are sending to your website? If you're taking this time and this money to run ads to your site, what do you think that your website is kind of doing on the back end to back up all of that hard work in your campaigns?

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:43.000
Emily Foster: I just wanted to introduce myself real quick. I am a branding and website designer. I run a marketing agency for wedding pros and creative businesses. I actually used to work for, like, bigger ad agencies and studied advertising, so some of my first work in the design industry was.

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:59.000
Emily Foster: designing creative for, like, Meta and Google Ads, um, and getting to have some of that background. And we kind of do everything except for paid advertising now as a studio, so I really love partnering with Whitney and Daniela, um, because they are amazing.

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:17.000
Emily Foster: in all of the ad strategy. I also am a past bride. I got married now four years ago, which kind of sounds like a lifetime ago, but because it, like, it was just such a different time, as you know, in the wedding industry. Um, but going through that process as a bride really helped me to see your client side. We've been in business five years.

00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:34.000
Emily Foster: And then I'm also that kind of age range of a zillennial, like Gen Z meets millennial, so I have tons of friends still getting married, going through that selection process of finding the right vendors. Um, I like to share, too, I'm an analytical buyer and a natural connector, so, like, in terms of when I'm buying something, I'm not typically the person that just sees an ad.

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:50.000
Emily Foster: ad and buys it right away. I usually probably am the person that you have to, like, use, like, retargeting strategies on and things like that, like, so I am going to be one that takes a lot longer, and I think that helps really well when we're writing copy for our clients and designing content, um, to kind of be in that mindset.

00:24:50.000 --> 00:25:12.000
Emily Foster: Um, love connecting with people in the industry. Our brands tend to be a mix of neutral and colorful, but they're all strategy-based, so that's what I mean when I say color lover, is that we really strive to make brands different. Um, but really, it comes back to your strategy as a business, and not just necessarily designing in, like, beige and black and white and calligraphy kind of styles.

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:32.000
Emily Foster: And then I also have, um, the Engage Your Brand podcast. There's a QR code here. I've had Whitney and Daniela on, and we've had a great conversation about advertising your wedding business. Um, so you can check out that episode. There's tons of episodes on here, too, that dive into lots of themes around marketing and branding, copywriting.

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:38.000
Emily Foster: all of the kind of themes that we're talking about today, so feel free to dive in there. I know we have, like, kind of a shorter time to cover everything today.

00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:55.000
Emily Foster: Um, but like I mentioned, I've been in business for 5 years, we've worked with over 100 wedding pros in that time, um, we know strategic, timeless, effective design, so when we're building your website, it's really about more than trends, it's really about building this strategic foundation that can withstand the test of time.

00:25:55.000 --> 00:26:10.000
Emily Foster: And yes, you do need to refresh a little bit every year in terms of new pricing and new galleries and things like that. But really, building your brand means building this evergreen brand that can grow with you for 10-plus years.

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:31.000
Emily Foster: Um, so it's really about building something based in strategy versus following trends and having you have to do it again every few years. Um, and I had about 10 years of experience total in the industry, um, I guess 12 years now. So, um, working with big businesses and small businesses before the wedding industry, so I bring a lot of that expertise into this conversation of.

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:41.000
Emily Foster: again, talking about more than what just works in the wedding industry. It's about understanding your ideal clients, but also just understanding, like, the actual science and psychology behind all of this.

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:59.000
Emily Foster: Okay, so quick health tech on your marketing. Um, I want you to open a recent ad, your website, and an organic social media profile. So it could be, like, um, a TikTok profile, Instagram, like, it doesn't even have to be somewhere that you are running ads, um, but, like, just opening up.

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:17.000
Emily Foster: these three different views for your business, and then start by asking yourself a couple questions. Do they look like they all came from the same business? And then, does each piece have a clear call to action for what to do next? Because one thing that we see a lot on websites is that.

00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:34.000
Emily Foster: yes, your ads have a call to action, but your website isn't actually telling people what to do next. It's just kind of like this landing place that you're getting people to, and then people are kind of confused about where to go next. Or even on organic profiles, one thing that I see is, like, it's a beautiful showcase of your work.

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:58.000
Emily Foster: But you're getting them to your Instagram profile, and then there's not a lot of clarity about where to go next. So being intentional about driving traffic from each of those sources to the next step is super important. And then also making sure that everything feels the same, because we talked about that bait and switch kind of mentality. If you're running ads and they feel like a totally different vibe than your website, that's going to be a huge disconnect with your clients.

00:27:58.000 --> 00:28:13.000
Emily Foster: I also do this exercise and even have you pull out a print material, so like a business card or a brochure, and see if that feels alike, too, because in-person marketing still matters, even in 2026, so making sure that everything feels really cohesive there.

00:28:13.000 --> 00:28:32.000
Emily Foster: Um, and if this… if you basically say no to these answers, that's totally normal. That's, uh, I think a really common thing in the industry that people struggle with, of not having clear call to actions, not having that brand consistency. But this is a nice health check to really just ask yourself these simple questions. Like, we'll dive into a lot of nitty-gritty things that you can update.

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:41.000
Emily Foster: But looking at the overall view of, is this all working together as kind of this whole ecosystem for my business?

00:28:42.000 --> 00:29:02.000
Emily Foster: So your marketing plan can't only be ads and also your marketing plan can't only be your brand and website. One thing that I love about partnering with me and Daniela is that people actually put effort and energy into attracting traffic to their website after because organic SEO is really powerful and so is organic social media, but it can only do so much at the end of the day.

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:17.000
Emily Foster: And if you really want to go far and future proof your business, you have to have this entire kind of like diverse plan of putting all of your different marketing eggs in different baskets and making sure that it's like running like a well oiled machine. So.

00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:27.000
Emily Foster: letting your website just set and then forgetting it isn't going to get you as far as implementing all of the right things on it and then running ads. So they really work hand-in-hand as partners.

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:49.000
Emily Foster: And your website isn't just pretty design, it's strategy. That's why I am so heavy on the strategy and not as heavy on specific styles for wedding businesses because it really has to work for your clients and you have to go through a strategic process. And a pretty design is so common in the industry. Yes, there are a lot of websites that are bare or.

00:29:49.000 --> 00:30:06.000
Emily Foster: not the right vibes, but the other thing that I see a ton are these beautiful websites that just aren't super functional. And so, if you really want to stand out, you have to have that kind of mix of attractive design that speaks to your client, but then also really strategy-focused content.

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:19.000
Emily Foster: So we're going to talk about the five pillars of an elevated website. I put these pillars together several years ago because I was thinking about the entire ecosystem for your website design and how all of that needs to work together.

00:30:19.000 --> 00:30:40.000
Emily Foster: So, when you're thinking about your website, it's not just design, not just branding, and not just, like, the words on the website, but it's how everything works together. So, hopefully you all can see this. But basically, the five pillars are strategy, content, copywriting, SEO, and design. In no particular order, there are a lot of different ways that you can work on this.

00:30:40.000 --> 00:31:03.000
Emily Foster: You definitely need to start with strategy and then the rest can kind of work together cohesively as you develop it. And when we work with clients on their website design, we're touching all of these like touch points, even if they book like one of our like lower projects, like a template customization. We're still applying best on-page SEO practices. We're giving them a copywriting guide so that they're.

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:23.000
Emily Foster: thinking strategically about their copy. We're designing the website. We're helping them think about content. And we're always starting with that strategy call. So everything comes together. And I really think that it does a disservice if you don't focus on all of them together. At some point there are definitely times where you can just invest in a brand to start.

00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:41.000
Emily Foster: But then you really want to think about the rest of these things when you're going into your website design. But what I often see is people kind of piecemealing when they're years in business, like, and this isn't a problem, this is a little bit different if you are, like, newer in business, so I want to give that caveat, but.

00:31:41.000 --> 00:31:56.000
Emily Foster: If you are, like, 10 plus years in business, and you're still kind of piecemealing, and you're like, I'm gonna hire a copywriter and work on that, and then maybe down the road, I'll hire a designer, you're really kind of wasting time, versus just looking at all of this strategically together, and working on it.

00:31:56.000 --> 00:32:03.000
Emily Foster: Um, as that whole ecosystem. I feel like I've been using that word a ton, but it really, like, works together as an entire system.

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:20.000
Emily Foster: So, we're going to review what each of those mean, um, but strategy is, like, I feel like it's this word that you hear a lot, especially if you've been in, like, in a coaching program or anything like that. Um, what I really mean by strategy is brand strategy. So, it's understanding the entire plan behind everything, and.

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:39.000
Emily Foster: how your business exists in the world. Um, one book that I really love is Building a Story Brand. It's a great, um, novel to talk about storytelling to people, and the way that we as humans follow stories and connect with others. Um, it's a really nice way to get in that… get, like, in your head about your brand, and the actual problems you solve in the world.

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:56.000
Emily Foster: But brand strategy also covers a lot of terms that you might have heard about, like ideal client profiles, target audience, demographics, and psychographics of your audience. And all of those things, when you do that work, translate super well to your ad strategy, because those are the things that you have to know for targeting the right people.

00:32:56.000 --> 00:33:16.000
Emily Foster: But then it also is, your market positioning, competitor analysis, brand archetypes, how your brand shows up in the world, your brand personality. So it really dives deeper than just asking yourself, like the why of your business or who your ideal clients are. You really have to think about how you exist in the market.

00:33:16.000 --> 00:33:35.000
Emily Foster: how you're differentiated, and how you're speaking to your clients. So, this could be an entire college course, or honestly, even an entire major, but just to touch on it, what you want to start with first is strategy. So, sitting down, ideally with an expert, and talking about your business, and where you want to go, and what you're trying to accomplish.

00:33:35.000 --> 00:33:50.000
Emily Foster: Um, or at least sitting down and workshopping with yourself if you are going to choose to kind of do this yourself. Um, but thinking strategically about all of those terms that I mentioned, writing them down. I have a workbook at the end, too, that's a good exercise to get you started on this process.

00:33:50.000 --> 00:34:07.000
Emily Foster: And I want you to focus on this, as hard as it can be for some people, before you even do anything design-related. So before you touch a color palette in Canva, or before you move any kind of pixel on your website, or try to write a line of copy for your website, I want you to focus on the strategy.

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:16.000
Emily Foster: And really take that time. Um, it's not as necessarily, like, sexy as it is to, like, just start developing a brand, but it's that really essential foundational work.

00:34:17.000 --> 00:34:32.000
Emily Foster: Next, after that, you want to focus on design. So we mentioned branding in the title, but design is the branding, and then it's also your website layout and aesthetics. And your brand design matters so much more than you think with how your website communicates.

00:34:32.000 --> 00:34:47.000
Emily Foster: to your clients. So, it's not just about having that nice logo at the top of the website, but it's about having this cohesive feel, because your website is just one part of your marketing, again. So, if you have those consistent brand guidelines, you'll have consistency that you can then apply to.

00:34:47.000 --> 00:35:03.000
Emily Foster: things like ad campaigns and print materials. So, you really want to do the work to develop a logo suite. Ideally, we like to develop logo suites that are at least, like, 2 or 3 logo designs, all the way up to sometimes we have clients who get, like, 15 logo designs in their packages.

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:15.000
Emily Foster: Because you want to make sure that you have the right logos that are communicating to the different markets that you have, um, different taglines, different formats. So, like, with this example, this is a design that we did for.

00:35:15.000 --> 00:35:30.000
Emily Foster: a stationer in Michigan, and we did monogram designs as part of their suite. You can also see the edge of a pattern design. So you want to invest in a logo suite, but then also have extra assets like patterns and icons that you can use on all of those digital and print materials.

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:38.000
Emily Foster: And then, as you're defining your brand, you'll also get brand guidelines. We'll talk about that a little bit later in this presentation.

00:35:38.000 --> 00:35:53.000
Emily Foster: But that will help define the colors, the consistent typography treatment, and all of that that needs to extend. And really, this is a huge part of that brand consistency from your digital ads to your website of like making sure that they have the same kind of feel.

00:35:53.000 --> 00:36:16.000
Emily Foster: This is also going to kind of influence the next part of the pillars, which is copywriting, and that's going to be the written voice of your website and the way that the words sound to your clients. So it's the actual text on your website, like headlines and body copy, which can then sometimes be used for your ads campaigns, too, like we've seen that a lot of times we can kind of copy and paste like the hard work.

00:36:16.000 --> 00:36:31.000
Emily Foster: and the money that a client has already invested in their copywriting, and the ads team can then, like, take it into ads and kind of tweak it and A-B test from there. So that's really great, and that's one of the reasons I think it saves a lot of time and performs really well.

00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:47.000
Emily Foster: Um, then the reason that we also have SEO as a pillar is because it plays a part in copywriting. So, you want to write for your ideal clients, but then there is the reality of writing for Google and AI searches, so SEO and GEO is kind of the way that that pillar has shifted, but.

00:36:47.000 --> 00:36:55.000
Emily Foster: You want to think strategically about the way that you're speaking to your clients, and then also making sure that you're getting the right keywords and the copywriting on your website.

00:36:55.000 --> 00:37:11.000
Emily Foster: And then as part of building this like strategic foundation, part of that strategy process is understanding how your business sounds in the world and how it exists. So you want to focus on building a guide with messaging that is authentic about the kind of words that you use.

00:37:11.000 --> 00:37:30.000
Emily Foster: Which, authentic is a great example of that, because we have brands that would never use that word that we work with, and then we have brands where it's like, no, authentic is the right word. So, it's getting really particular about the kind of wording that you use for your clients, and the kind of voice, and establishing that. So, ideally, a messaging guide is part of this process, and.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:41.000
Emily Foster: Directing that, but then it's also the actual copywriting that goes into your website, the wording that you would use across your other marketing materials, and the wording that you continue to use in your campaigns.

00:37:41.000 --> 00:38:03.000
Emily Foster: And then SEO, so I mentioned SEO, I should have added GEO here too. So that's like AI searches, like coming up in ChatGPT and Claude and Gemini and other AI searches. So this is how people are finding you in one way. Obviously, you also have your ads campaigns and then also your organic social media and in person and all of that.

00:38:03.000 --> 00:38:27.000
Emily Foster: But I really think that you're doing a disservice if you don't at least consider organic SEO as part of your strategy, because it's actually so easy to get done during this process, and so easy to optimize your site well, so that you can bring in some organic leads through Google. But again, we do want to also focus on the ad strategy, because you can get more leads that way. And SEO is going to be a long game. So.

00:38:27.000 --> 00:38:44.000
Emily Foster: That's the other reason that I like to mention it as part of the pillars, is that if you get the foundation laid out today, then you can see results from it in, like, six to nine months. But this is how people find you, and it's not just for bloggers or for large businesses. We can apply the right strategies in a way.

00:38:44.000 --> 00:39:00.000
Emily Foster: This includes doing keyword research based on the strategy that we've done and understanding who your ideal clients are because not all keywords are relevant to your business. You want to be really intentional about how you're applying those. And then you want to include the best practices on the website.

00:39:01.000 --> 00:39:21.000
Emily Foster: And that brings me to pillar 5, which is content. It's basically, like, everything that doesn't work on your website as your sales copywriting, or your conversion copywriting, or, like, the traditional design that you would think about. But this is content that I think can be super powerful for your ads campaign, so it's super important to consider it, too.

00:39:21.000 --> 00:39:39.000
Emily Foster: Basically, blogging, galleries, and lead magnets kind of fall into this. And it can also be FAQs, which are also very important with enhancing your SEO and GEO. But it's thinking strategically about what kind of blogs do you want to write for your site? How do you want to showcase the right content?

00:39:39.000 --> 00:40:06.000
Emily Foster: And then these kinds of tools on your website actually help to build your domain authority and give search engines something worth ranking. So you really want to be intentional about how it all comes together. Lead magnets, I think, can be a really great way to run ads. So obviously you're probably running a lot of ads to your home page or a landing page, maybe a contact page, but then also having some resources built out can be a great way to.

00:40:06.000 --> 00:40:12.000
Emily Foster: Um, have another landing point for ads, or continue to drive some organic traffic there, too.

00:40:13.000 --> 00:40:34.000
Emily Foster: Okay, so I wanted to share some essentials and then also some like hiccups that I see with conversions on your website. So when we think of someone landing on your website, the reasons that they may click out or the reasons that they might convert really well. So the number one thing is a strong brand that connects, like coming back to that kind of foundation that we were talking about with the design pillar of.

00:40:34.000 --> 00:40:55.000
Emily Foster: aesthetics that make sense for your ideal clients, which you find out what aesthetics make sense through the strategy process, but making sure that it looks visually appealing to your clients and that it's clean, easy to follow, and isn't something that feels trendy or like it's like so last year. So that's why we want to build this like evergreen brand.

00:40:55.000 --> 00:41:10.000
Emily Foster: Evergreen doesn't always mean, like, timeless, and all of these words are kind of subjective of what they mean, so it doesn't mean having to build, like, a black and white, um, like, calligraphy-style brand. That's why I really like to kind of push the envelope with the aesthetics that we're working with.

00:41:10.000 --> 00:41:18.000
Emily Foster: But it does mean building something that stands out, um, and isn't based on trends, it's more based on strategies.

00:41:18.000 --> 00:41:41.000
Emily Foster: and then easy navigation at the top and bottom of your website. So we included desktop examples here. But this matters a ton for mobile as well. You want to make sure that mobile tablet and desktop are optimized. But what I mean by like an easy navigation at the top and bottom of your site is a menu that's super easy to skim. It doesn't always have to be this kind of traditional menu with like logo and menu items.

00:41:41.000 --> 00:41:56.000
Emily Foster: You can get creative, but you do want to make sure that it's really clear where people can contact you right away. You want to make sure it's easy for them to find information about you, easy to get to your services and packages and pricing, and don't make them work terribly hard for that.

00:41:56.000 --> 00:42:11.000
Emily Foster: Another tip that we also do is having a call to action. So, like, maybe you have the top navigation, but then also have a call to action very quickly, high up on the page. And that can look different for each site. So, like, in this example, we do have a button here that leads to the contact page.

00:42:11.000 --> 00:42:27.000
Emily Foster: In this site, we don't have a button right at the top, we have the gallery, but then if you just scroll once, there is a call to action right below that. So you can get creative with how that looks, but you just want to make sure that it's really quick and easy for people to get that next step on your website.

00:42:27.000 --> 00:42:43.000
Emily Foster: And then the next one is clear messaging. So you want your website to work for different buyer types. I mentioned that I'm more of an analytical buyer. So I'm the kind of person that will read like almost every paragraph of copy on a website, especially if I'm getting ready to spend multiple thousands of dollars.

00:42:43.000 --> 00:42:59.000
Emily Foster: So, you want to make sure that the body copy that supports your headlines works for those kinds of buyers. But then you also, in reality, have buyers who are just going to skim. Like, my husband's the kind of person that he'll just, like, read a couple headlines, and then hit contact, and…

00:42:59.000 --> 00:43:17.000
Emily Foster: maybe ask questions later, or maybe just make the investment. And so, for those people that are, like, quicker to buy, you want to make sure that the headlines and the sub-headlines work well. So, it's about having really clear connection with your clients through sub-headlines and headlines, but then also having the supporting copy on the website.

00:43:17.000 --> 00:43:27.000
Emily Foster: And then having clear call to actions or those buttons in pretty much every section of your website so that it gives different buyers an opportunity to contact you.

00:43:27.000 --> 00:43:45.000
Emily Foster: Um, and then best SEO practices. So, doing the keyword research based on your strategy, applying those keywords to the places like your headlines, um, your page titles, and meta descriptions. We… I can totally answer questions about this if any of you have questions. Um, but optimizing each page of your website for the keywords.

00:43:45.000 --> 00:43:47.000
Emily Foster: that came up in your strategy.

00:43:49.000 --> 00:44:04.000
Emily Foster: All right. And then common friction points that I see that can be really frustrating. Again, even if you've attracted someone through an ad and they're on your website, so it feels like you've done like the hard work of getting them there. These things can make someone click out pretty quickly.

00:44:04.000 --> 00:44:28.000
Emily Foster: Any pop ups that are annoying, this happens a little bit more often than you would think, especially on mobile. But a pop up with like a very tiny X in the corner or like no X at all is going to have someone close out of your website entirely. So you have to think about that 0.05 seconds, especially in the wedding industry where people probably have multiple tabs open on their website. So it could be that they saw an ad for your business.

00:44:28.000 --> 00:44:44.000
Emily Foster: But then they also got some referrals from their planner, and they've found some people in organic Google search, and so often you're, like, one of many tabs that they have open on their website. So any little thing, like a pop-up, even if it seems, like, not a big deal to us, is going to have them quickly.

00:44:44.000 --> 00:44:54.000
Emily Foster: click out and go to the next option on their browser. A lack of call to actions on a page, so no buttons, no clear navigation getting them to the next step.

00:44:54.000 --> 00:45:15.000
Emily Foster: copywriting that doesn't align with a client, so anything that they're reading that sounds, like, cliche, and cliche is subjective, so this is where it really matters to think through your strategy and your brand voice and what makes sense for your clients. Um, but things that sound cliche, that don't sound like them, if they can't see themselves in the copywriting, or if they can't see themselves in the images, and.

00:45:15.000 --> 00:45:22.000
Emily Foster: They kind of feel like there's not a place for them in your business based on your website, then that's going to be something that has them clicking out.

00:45:22.000 --> 00:45:40.000
Emily Foster: branding that doesn't align with the client. So, um, this can go both ways. It could be that your brand doesn't feel high-end enough for them, which is a common thing we know. Um, but then it also could be that your website and your brand feel too high-end, and, like, almost not approachable for them. So, you really want to be clear about.

00:45:40.000 --> 00:45:49.000
Emily Foster: this in your strategy process of what is your pricing like? Where do you sit in the market? And how can you make your branding make sense for your ideal client?

00:45:49.000 --> 00:46:11.000
Emily Foster: links that don't work. So this goes along with call to actions. But anything like email links, even where they try to click on your email. And then it's not very quick to email you or a contact form that doesn't submit or a navigation that doesn't open. So making sure that you do that kind of health check to make sure that everything's functioning properly, especially on Mobile.

00:46:11.000 --> 00:46:21.000
Emily Foster: Because I think that's the place that we really kind of forget to check. Like, we'll be on our computers, and we check all of the main pages there, but then we don't do the work to make sure that the mobile site is working for us.

00:46:21.000 --> 00:46:38.000
Emily Foster: And then text-heavy sections or sections that are too image-heavy. So there's kind of this concept when you're scrolling through a site where eventually content starts to feel like just like a wall of the same thing. So one thing that we intentionally try to do with our designs is balancing copy and images so that.

00:46:38.000 --> 00:46:59.000
Emily Foster: It's breathable and also like balancing negative space. So that happens by having headlines in the right spots, having paragraphs be certain lengths and then like spacing things out with images. And this is such a nuanced part of the process that goes into that design pillar. It can be really hard to get it done perfectly. It's not like a black or white rule of like saying.

00:46:59.000 --> 00:47:16.000
Emily Foster: every paragraph should have less than 5 sentences, or every headline should be, like, less than 10 words, or anything like that. Um, like, people try to ask, like, how many images should I have on my website? It's not a black and white rule like that, it's just being really intentional about how each section looks.

00:49:10.000 --> 00:49:34.000
Emily Foster: billion dollar brands that I showed at the beginning, like Coca-Cola or Starbucks or Home Depot, like, they're never changing up the shades of colors that they use. It's always staying consistent. So direction on how to use those, access to your font files, and the way that you should treat everything, basically. And then even, we'll sometimes include, like, photography direction and those messaging guides for copywriting so that you have that consistency.

00:49:35.000 --> 00:49:58.000
Emily Foster: So brand guidelines, again, give you those logo variations, the font pairings that work well together, exact color codes that you can use so that you're not just guessing how to design creative for everything. And then also that style direction for if you are going to be doing like brand shoots, styled shoots, and then also ads, like understanding like what kind of photos are going to work best for this brand.

00:49:58.000 --> 00:50:12.000
Emily Foster: even if it's a photo that you have from a wedding, it doesn't always mean that it's the best photo to use for your strategy. So being really clear about, like, how are we showcasing your business, and what kind of content goes along with the right strategy that we're going for.

00:50:12.000 --> 00:50:28.000
Emily Foster: And then consistent messaging and voice. So it's deeper than just keywords that you're using for like your SEO or GEO or your ad strategy. It's understanding what kind of words sound like you, whether they're like location-based words that you're using or.

00:50:28.000 --> 00:50:36.000
Emily Foster: Kind of stylistic choices, and understanding, like, how you speak to your clients, and then how your ads campaigns need to speak as a result.

00:50:37.000 --> 00:50:57.000
Emily Foster: And then you want to position your brand for that lead generation, again, based in strategy. So before you even start working on logo concepts or picking colors, you really want to do the groundwork to ask about the why of your business and dive deeper into that brand strategy. So I mentioned that Building a Story brand book.

00:50:57.000 --> 00:51:09.000
Emily Foster: And some of those details that you really want to focus on for your brand strategy, but asking yourself those questions. I have a workbook in just a couple pages that I'll share with you that you can use to kind of start with that groundwork.

00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:24.000
Emily Foster: And then create the brand that reflects your strategy from there, and then extend that into your ads campaigns. Um, so then after that, like, you want to extend it to your organic SEO, which will greatly help feed your ad strategy.

00:51:24.000 --> 00:51:45.000
Emily Foster: Alright, um, this is kind of the end. I wanted to wrap it up quickly. I feel like I stuffed a lot into the hour, but I wanted to have time for Q&A. Um, but if you're interested in building this foundation so that your ads perform even better, um, I would love to work with you. You can find me at Emily Foster Creative on Instagram, Facebook, and Pinterest, and then this is my contact info.

00:51:45.000 --> 00:52:01.000
Emily Foster: And then the resources I mentioned. So I have the elevated website checklist. This is going to be a checklist that has more of those details of the do's and don'ts for your website and what to practice on your website. So that QR code is here, and I can send these links to Whitney and Daniela as well.

00:52:01.000 --> 00:52:21.000
Emily Foster: Um, and then there's also this brand clarity workbook that I mentioned a couple times. Um, that's kind of like a journaling exercise to get you started thinking about your brand strategy. Again, I think it's great to at least go through the process at some point, whether it is with, like, your ads agency or with the branding studio that you work with.

00:52:21.000 --> 00:52:26.000
Emily Foster: Um, but just making sure that you're talking it out with someone who can build that foundation for you.

00:52:26.000 --> 00:52:48.000
Emily Foster: So there's those two resources. And then I also have this resource. If you want a website audit, we have website audits available for $300. It's an hour long, like kind of coaching style call that we record to go through your website. And then we also have one off and retainer services and you can contact me at that contact link if you want to talk about working together.

00:52:50.000 --> 00:52:56.000
Whitney Hodges: Amazing, Emily. Thank you so much. There was so much jam-packed in there that was so valuable.

00:52:55.000 --> 00:53:03.000
Emily Foster: I'm glad. I felt like I was moving quickly. I know it's a lot to cover, but hopefully this gets you all thinking about how to convert your website better.

00:53:03.000 --> 00:53:16.000
Whitney Hodges: And thanks for sharing all the resources, too. We can definitely include the links to those in the email that we send out after this, along with the recording from the replay.

00:53:09.000 --> 00:53:11.000
Emily Foster: Yeah.

00:53:14.000 --> 00:53:16.000
Emily Foster: Okay.

00:53:16.000 --> 00:53:34.000
Whitney Hodges: For all of our Accelerator clients, also, this is all gonna get added to the Vault, so you can reference it back at any time, um, and reference back all of these workbooks and resources as well. Um, so if anybody has any questions, feel free to drop those, um.

00:53:34.000 --> 00:53:50.000
Whitney Hodges: In the chat, and we're happy to help with those. Um, but this definitely has my wheels spinning on, like, all of the things, right? So, like, a lot of you in the room probably know, like, 9 times out of 10, usually we are running ads directly to a landing page.

00:53:50.000 --> 00:54:05.000
Whitney Hodges: As opposed to, like, the full-blown website, right? But what is so interesting, or, I mean, maybe it's kind of obvious, right? The creation of those landing pages is so much faster and so much easier when.

00:54:05.000 --> 00:54:20.000
Whitney Hodges: the website is already… and the brand are already in amazing spots, right? So, like, to your point, Emily, when there are already brand guidelines that exist that are super clear, super thorough, really do, like, lay out who that ideal client is.

00:54:20.000 --> 00:54:37.000
Whitney Hodges: it's just a matter of, honestly, kind of, like, just cherry-picking and, like, copying a lot from the existing website into a long-form landing page that we know is going to convert well for ads. And I feel like one of the best ways for me to convey this, honestly, would be, like.

00:54:27.000 --> 00:54:28.000
Emily Foster: Mm-hmm.

00:54:37.000 --> 00:54:53.000
Whitney Hodges: In reflecting on some of our accelerator clients and kind of like where they've been at in their their process, right? Because usually one of the the very first things that we do when we welcome people into the accelerators is we give them all of our curriculum on how they can kind of like.

00:54:53.000 --> 00:55:10.000
Whitney Hodges: audit their social media, their website, kind of their assets that they have as a whole to make sure that they're ready for ads traffic, and it gives them kind of a breakdown on what's going to make a great landing page for their ads. And we'll see some clients who spend.

00:55:10.000 --> 00:55:26.000
Whitney Hodges: honestly, like, a couple of months needing to, like, go through that curriculum to, like, build the landing page. It can be more of, like, a painstaking process for them, versus… I'm thinking of, like, a past client of yours who needed to create an ads traffic landing page.

00:55:26.000 --> 00:55:42.000
Whitney Hodges: And she did it in a day, right? Because, like, all of that, like, already existed. So all of that foundational stuff, I mean, it's just so important because everything works together so holistically, right? Because even if an ad is directing people to a landing page and not.

00:55:42.000 --> 00:56:03.000
Whitney Hodges: the website, your business is getting more visibility in general. So many of today's couples are analytical buyers just like you are, Emily, that just because the ad didn't necessarily direct them to the full-blown website, they're still going to make their way over to the website through the research process, right? And it's so important that everything really is built for those conversions.

00:55:57.000 --> 00:55:59.000
Emily Foster: Okay.

00:56:04.000 --> 00:56:18.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, yeah, that's such a good point, because I feel like sometimes, like, if you are doing the heavy work for the landing page, but they haven't done the work for the rest of their website, there's that disconnect, too, where people will be like, well, why does this one page look so different from everything else?

00:56:14.000 --> 00:56:15.000
Whitney Hodges: Mmhm.

00:56:18.000 --> 00:56:20.000
Whitney Hodges: Exactly. Yeah.

00:56:19.000 --> 00:56:20.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, I…

00:56:21.000 --> 00:56:42.000
Whitney Hodges: So Mona had a really good question. She said, any suggestions on strategy when trying to attract different audiences? She says, we plan weddings as well as corporate, nonprofit, government, and other social events. And even within the wedding part of our business, we also plan Indian and South Asian, which would be different than like just a more.

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:31.000
Emily Foster: Mm-hmm.

00:56:42.000 --> 00:56:43.000
Whitney Hodges: Traditional.

00:56:42.000 --> 00:57:07.000
Emily Foster: Yeah. Yeah, I think that's where it is really important to get clear about, like, your page strategy, which I didn't really talk a ton about, like, UX and, like, the overall, like, sitemap strategy for your website, but we have a lot of clients that do have, like, different focuses like that. Like, they might have different audiences within the wedding industry, or they do, like, like, social and corporate and nonprofit and weddings.

00:57:07.000 --> 00:57:35.000
Emily Foster: So I would say, like, having a dedicated page for most audiences is going to be preferable. But it is kind of knowing, like, I wouldn't necessarily say that you need, like, a separate page for, like, South Asian weddings. Like, in that case, like, you would want to just make sure that you're clear with your messaging and your images that you are capable of doing those larger weddings and, like, speaking to the pain points of those kinds of clients somewhere. But it's, like, not as literal as, like, in your navigation having, like.

00:57:35.000 --> 00:57:55.000
Emily Foster: Um, like a weddings tab and then it drops down and it's like South Asian weddings and like, like, I don't know, stereotypical weddings or something like that. Um, so you want to get kind of strategic about the messaging, but then also like in your site map that makes sure that you have like different services, um, indicated, especially when it is like those totally different worlds of like corporate versus weddings.

00:57:56.000 --> 00:58:15.000
Emily Foster: Um, and then in your SEO strategy, think about that as well, like, doing keyword research for each audience. Um, and then I would, like, this is kind of more of a basic kind of answer after all of that, like, all of that is a lot more complex than it sounds, like, it does take more time and thought of, like, thinking through, like, okay, should we have this page or this page, and, like.

00:58:15.000 --> 00:58:32.000
Emily Foster: having that kind of conversation about what's most important. But then, once you do that work of creating everything, like, looking at it through the lens of each of your ideal clients, and thinking about, like, okay, if I was a corporate client, and I landed on this website, how quickly can I find the information that I need? Or…

00:58:32.000 --> 00:58:47.000
Emily Foster: If I am an Indian couple and I'm like looking to find the right wedding planner, like how quickly do I see myself in the messaging and like what's my next call to action and kind of go through that client journey on your website to make sure that you're speaking to all of those clients.

00:58:48.000 --> 00:59:05.000
Emily Foster: Um, and the other thing, like, is you might need to create separate brands if it is distinct enough, like, um, that is a possibility sometimes. We don't always have clients do that, but I do have clients who have created, like, different websites for two different audiences, which is kind of, like.

00:59:05.000 --> 00:59:09.000
Emily Foster: the most extreme side. I think that gets the most heavy lifting.

00:59:09.000 --> 00:59:27.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah, we oftentimes will see different landing pages for these, like, different kind of sectors, different types of events, and then that is where your ad strategy can get more layered as well, right? Like, Google ad campaigns specific for your corporate events, specific for your holiday parties, specific for your…

00:59:21.000 --> 00:59:22.000
Emily Foster: Hi.

00:59:27.000 --> 00:59:30.000
Whitney Hodges: your weddings.

00:59:28.000 --> 00:59:29.000
Emily Foster: Yeah.

00:59:30.000 --> 00:59:37.000
Whitney Hodges: Does a blogging strategy kind of play into that as well at all, Emily? Like, blog posts that are, like, dedicated to those different types of events?

00:59:37.000 --> 00:59:57.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, definitely, like, I even have a client who has two, like, it's one blog, like, they have a Showit website, and then a blog that runs through it, um, and we designed her blog pages, this is another reason I love, like, we didn't talk about platforms today, but I love Showit, because you can't really do this in platforms like Wix, or Squarespace, or WordPress as easily, but.

00:59:57.000 --> 01:00:26.000
Emily Foster: We have the blog page designed so that when she has couples and then she also has high school seniors as her ideal clients. So when a senior goes to the blog, they're going to only see content relevant to them. And then if a couple goes to the blog, they're only going to see wedding and engagement kind of content. So it does go with your SEO strategy with the keywords that you're finding. You would want to find keywords that are great for blog topics for each audience. And then you can lay out the blogs in a way.

01:00:26.000 --> 01:00:32.000
Emily Foster: that are really easy for each of those clients to digest.

01:00:32.000 --> 01:00:43.000
Whitney Hodges: Awesome. I love that. Okay, well, if anybody else has any questions, feel free. I mean, you can unmute yourself. You can raise your hand if you'd like. You can pop it in the chat if you'd like.

01:00:38.000 --> 01:00:40.000
Emily Foster: Alright.

01:00:43.000 --> 01:01:00.000
Whitney Hodges: I think the one thing that I would be interested to hear more about from you, Emily, is just kind of the methodology of like the bridge of what happens between the strategy and then getting to the actual like.

01:01:00.000 --> 01:01:18.000
Whitney Hodges: design of the brand. Because for us as ad strategists, right, people are coming to us typically with, like, the brand guidelines are already done. We're just following the brand guidelines sort of thing, so we don't do quite that piece of it. Um, but I'm interested to hear, like, your process, right, of how, like, okay, if this is the strategy.

01:01:07.000 --> 01:01:08.000
Emily Foster: Mm-hmm.

01:01:18.000 --> 01:01:21.000
Whitney Hodges: How do we end up with this is what the brand looks like?

01:01:21.000 --> 01:01:46.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, yeah, so we do a call initially with clients, like, we'll go on a strategy call, and then, like, internally, our team takes the strategy notes and starts working on a mood board for clients, and our mood boards are, like, way more extensive than, like, I feel like, you know, you might see, like, a planner's mood board, and it's, like, one page kind of thing that shows the vibe, and some designers do it that way too, but usually ours are, like, anywhere from, like, three to seven plus pages.

01:01:46.000 --> 01:02:04.000
Emily Foster: So there's, like, a page that talks about the messaging, if we're writing their copy, and there's, like, a page that talks about, um, the overall design decisions that we want to make for the brand, and then the website style. Uh, so we pick, like, their color palette, um, based in the strategy, and that, like, goes, um, like, we factor in color psychology, and…

01:02:04.000 --> 01:02:28.000
Emily Foster: Like we do take a look at like the market and like how we can make people stand out compared to their competitors. So the mood board is like such a huge part of it. And then we send that over to the client for feedback. And I always tell people like to do a gut check of like, do you like this? But then also do you think it works for your ideal clients? And we have like feedback forms throughout each step of the process that like asks you those intentional questions to be like.

01:02:28.000 --> 01:02:45.000
Emily Foster: Um, like, do you like it? Do you approve it? But then also, do you think this works for your strategy and your ideal clients? And, like, kind of doing that check each time? Um, because even if it works for your strategy and your ideal client, I don't want people to end up with, like, a brand or website that they hate and that they can't stand. So there is kind of that happy medium.

01:02:45.000 --> 01:03:03.000
Emily Foster: So the mood board and kind of like stylistic selection process is such a huge part. And that's before we've even like opened Adobe to design logos. And then once we get on the same page about the mood board, from there we put together a brand presentation. So that includes like logo designs, patterns, typography selection.

01:03:03.000 --> 01:03:23.000
Emily Foster: Uh, and, like, icons, so that's, like, where we are taking the hours to, like, design everything and bring it together, and then we present that to the client and show them, like, their logos in every kind of different color that we could have, like, um, we'll show them, like, the patterns and the icons and lay it all out in a cohesive presentation, and then we go through the same kind of feedback.

01:03:23.000 --> 01:03:42.000
Emily Foster: process of, like, um, do you like this? Does this work for your strategy? Like, are there any edits that you want to see? And then we go through revisions. Um, revisions, I feel like, are a nice thing to talk about, too, because, um, people sometimes are weary to give revisions. Like, I think they're kind of like, oh, I don't want to hurt your feelings, or something like that, but.

01:03:42.000 --> 01:03:59.000
Emily Foster: Um, it's such a normal part of the process, and I would say we probably have about, like, 25 to 30% of our clients, like, give, like, semi-extensive revisions as well, and we do have, like, a lot of clients just approve things right away, but that revision and kind of collaboration process is such a…

01:03:59.000 --> 01:04:16.000
Emily Foster: important part of the process. Um, so we go through that, and then we export the logos, put together brand guidelines, and then from there, we start designing the website, and we'll design the homepage to start, and run that by with the same feedback process, and then go through the rest of the page designs, and.

01:04:16.000 --> 01:04:26.000
Emily Foster: Present the website as a whole, and make sure that we do, like, the final checks, like, tech checks and everything, make sure there's no broken links, and then we go into launch from there.

01:04:26.000 --> 01:04:34.000
Whitney Hodges: Love it. Okay, makes sense. So, one thing that came to mind for me is sometimes I feel like we'll see…

01:04:34.000 --> 01:04:38.000
Whitney Hodges: Certain wedding pros going through rebrands.

01:04:38.000 --> 01:04:54.000
Whitney Hodges: I don't know, like, every few years or so, almost, like, thinking that the branding is, like, the issue, and so, like, oh, I need to, like, rebrand sort of thing. But what would you say, like, when you're really doing the branding right, and, like, addressing the foundational pieces of it, like.

01:04:41.000 --> 01:04:42.000
Emily Foster: No.

01:04:47.000 --> 01:04:49.000
Emily Foster: Yeah.

01:04:54.000 --> 01:05:10.000
Whitney Hodges: I would think that that is going to serve you for a really long time, right? Like, do you know if… I mean, is there, like, a shelf life that you would say of, like, when your branding is really done right, like, it's going to work for you forever, sort of thing? Or is it, like, 10 years, or…

01:05:08.000 --> 01:05:32.000
Emily Foster: Uh, yeah. Yeah, I would think like at least 10 to 20 years and like the caveat being like a big brand strategy overhaul, like if you are starting to attract a different niche, um, like Mona's question earlier was a great example. Like if you are like a planner and you, maybe you used to work tons of like South Asian weddings and then.

01:05:10.000 --> 01:05:12.000
Whitney Hodges: What would you say?

01:05:32.000 --> 01:05:47.000
Emily Foster: you kind of get to a point in your business where you're like, I never want to take another wedding like that again, like, I only want to do elopements, and that would be, like, brand repositioning that probably does require some kind of… at least a brand refresh, and we can talk about, like, the difference, kind of, between a rebrand and brand refresh, but…

01:05:39.000 --> 01:05:40.000
Whitney Hodges: Great.

01:05:47.000 --> 01:05:48.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

01:05:47.000 --> 01:06:08.000
Emily Foster: Um, as long as, like, the name of your business is still working for you, and you're still, like, speaking to the same clients, and you're still kind of in, like, the same tier of market, your brand should work for you for, like, 10 to 20 plus years. Um, and that's, like, I always like talking about the big corporate brands, because you've seen, like, um, like, Coca-Cola or, like, Starbucks logos change a little bit over time, but, like.

01:06:03.000 --> 01:06:04.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

01:06:08.000 --> 01:06:25.000
Emily Foster: If you Google their brand from the 80s, it still looks pretty similar and still has a similar vibe. You can modernize things a little bit, but ideally, you're not having to modernize that much in that short of a time frame because you're not relying on trends.

01:06:13.000 --> 01:06:14.000
Whitney Hodges: Right.

01:06:15.000 --> 01:06:16.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay.

01:06:25.000 --> 01:06:42.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah, okay, got it. I think that that totally makes sense. Okay, so Sean asked an interesting question. Sean has an ice sculpture business here in Austin, but then you've got another location. Is it in St. Louis? Is that where your other arm of the business is, too? In San Antonio, too? Am I getting that right?

01:06:42.000 --> 01:06:56.000
stlic: Yeah, we're kind of in a few different markets, like Austin, San Antonio are the primary market here, and then we just opened a shop in St. Louis, and we're, uh, expanding into Kansas City a little bit.

01:06:56.000 --> 01:07:17.000
stlic: But I struggle with like my ad spend because if people are searching for ice sculptures, I'm already at the top in most of the markets just naturally. But it's like, how do I branch out to end, you know, trends like last year was espresso martinis, this year is champagne towers.

01:07:04.000 --> 01:07:05.000
Emily Foster: You are.

01:07:17.000 --> 01:07:28.000
stlic: marquee lighting has become popular the last couple years. It's kind of adjacent, where, like, if people want marquee lights, that might be a client who might.

01:07:28.000 --> 01:07:38.000
stlic: one ice sculpture, two. And so, like, I don't want to spend on Google Ads for people who are already searching ice sculptures, because there's not a ton.

01:07:38.000 --> 01:07:53.000
stlic: of competition, and I'm already showing up. But how do we expand that reach to people who don't know what they don't know, or are like, oh, I want a champagne tower at my wedding, because that's in…

01:07:53.000 --> 01:07:58.000
stlic: And then it's like, oh, well, you know, the espresso martini was so in last year.

01:07:58.000 --> 01:07:59.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, I…

01:07:58.000 --> 01:08:09.000
stlic: where did all those brides just move to the Champagne Tower this year? And so, that's kind of where I'm struggling at, is like, am I really getting the bang for my buck on this Google AdWords?

01:08:02.000 --> 01:08:03.000
Emily Foster: Okay.

01:08:09.000 --> 01:08:25.000
stlic: Um, I don't really have an e-comm kind of product, so, like, the meta ads aren't really ideal either, because I just need so much information. So, how do I get to those clients, or what's the strategy about, hey, if you're searching for.

01:08:26.000 --> 01:08:35.000
stlic: Beer Borough Company, you might also be interested in there or like, you know, any kind of like niche kind of off.

01:08:36.000 --> 01:08:39.000
stlic: adjacent, you know, kind of companies.

01:08:39.000 --> 01:08:45.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, totally. And, um, just to be clear, like, you don't necessarily do marquee lights or, like, champagne…

01:08:44.000 --> 01:08:55.000
stlic: We don't do that at all, no. I just… it's like, the mentality of that customer is like, hey, we wanna spice up our wedding, or our event, and…

01:08:45.000 --> 01:08:48.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, it just pairs well.

01:08:49.000 --> 01:08:51.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, I…

01:08:55.000 --> 01:09:11.000
stlic: we're gonna get flowers for sure, but, like, do all the table centerpieces need to be flowers? How do we get the… some ice in there? Or… I'm not trying to, like, undercut anybody else's business, or downplay their stuff, but…

01:09:09.000 --> 01:09:10.000
Emily Foster: Yeah. Okay.

01:09:11.000 --> 01:09:16.000
stlic: I do want to try and compete a little bit on those things that are…

01:09:14.000 --> 01:09:16.000
Emily Foster: One.

01:09:16.000 --> 01:09:21.000
stlic: ancillary to, like, what do you need for a wedding, you know?

01:09:20.000 --> 01:09:35.000
Emily Foster: Yeah. Yeah, that makes sense. First, that's such a cool business. I love that you do ice sculptures. And also, I feel like that's such a great problem to have, Whitney, of you're already at the top for ice sculptures. So I think it's great to think about this expansion.

01:09:35.000 --> 01:09:51.000
Emily Foster: Um, there's, like, a lot of ways that you could go about it, so I think that the blogging strategy could be really useful here, or, like, frequently asked questions, so getting some organic content on your website that does mention keywords like marquee lights or champagne towers and things like that.

01:09:51.000 --> 01:10:14.000
Emily Foster: I say that with organic, because I feel like I'd want like Whitney or Daniela to say like I like. I am cautious about you running ads specifically to a page that is ranking for marquee lights, because I'm worried about that getting into like false advertising. But it is okay. If it's more like organic content, like, if you were to write a blog post that's like.

01:10:14.000 --> 01:10:29.000
Emily Foster: how to amplify your marquee lights experience or something. And then in the blog post, you're mentioning, like, 10 kinds of ice sculptures. That would be really cool to complement with that. So it's not like you're advertising and selling marquee lights, but you're, like, selling the experience that goes along with it.

01:10:29.000 --> 01:10:45.000
Emily Foster: Um, but, like, we wouldn't want you ranking to say, like, marquee lights available, and then people click on an ad and get there, and then they're not there, because I feel like that could just get into, like, mostly legal issues in the future, um, or if anything, like, frustrated clients.

01:10:45.000 --> 01:11:01.000
Emily Foster: Um, so I think adjacent content that can work well, like doing some keyword research about those kinds of topics, um, and seeing, like, if they even rank well in your area, kind of thing, like, if it's worth the squeeze, um, to kind of, like, have those blog posts.

01:11:01.000 --> 01:11:24.000
Emily Foster: And then having tons of call to actions in the blog post that links out to your services or your options for purchasing the ice sculpture experiences. And then you could run ads to those blogs. That could be a strategy if you're already kind of maxing out what you're doing with landing pages around ice sculptures. You could run ads to blog posts that have that kind of supplemental content.

01:11:24.000 --> 01:11:42.000
Emily Foster: Um, and, like, have examples of how you work together with that. And then, just, like, another tip, kind of outside the whole, like, digital advertising or, like, digital online space, like, I think that you could also start collaborating with those companies if they don't already offer ice sculptures, but, like.

01:11:42.000 --> 01:11:59.000
Emily Foster: Think about the kind of strategic partnerships you could create and you could bring that over to your website strategy of basically having relationships with them where maybe you write a blog post for their website and they write one for yours and you give back links to each other to feature each other's services.

01:11:59.000 --> 01:12:10.000
Emily Foster: Um, or consider, like, creating a referral program with them, like, maybe you have, like, a special offer that you give to a local marquee light company if one of their clients books an ice sculpture.

01:12:10.000 --> 01:12:25.000
Emily Foster: Um, and then this isn't always, like, I feel like the best answer for everyone, because it does depend on, like, your overhead and, like, your interest in it, but you could also consider, like, getting those adjacent services under your umbrella if you don't want to kind of invest the time in the strategic partnerships, but.

01:12:25.000 --> 01:12:34.000
Emily Foster: Start offering marquee lights or champagne towers, and then you could obviously do the keyword research and start ranking content for those.

01:12:34.000 --> 01:12:44.000
stlic: Yeah, I want to be cautious of, like you said, I'm not trying to sell marquee lights or add a different business to my business, but…

01:12:34.000 --> 01:12:35.000
Emily Foster: Does that help?

01:12:40.000 --> 01:12:42.000
Emily Foster: Why?

01:12:44.000 --> 01:13:06.000
stlic: just identifying the trends and staying current with, like you said, probably the blog post kind of situation is probably gonna be my best bet there. yeah, it's just like I know people are searching for things. If they're searching for my things, we do have a pretty good closing rate on our like. If someone fills out a form to your point earlier.

01:12:48.000 --> 01:12:49.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, right.

01:13:06.000 --> 01:13:22.000
stlic: we're getting more qualified leads is, you know, and that's the thing, like, oh, when we did Meta stuff, we'd get a ton of leads, but they were all ghosts. And how much time do you spend on tracking that down versus, like, no, you have to fill out this lengthy form.

01:13:16.000 --> 01:13:17.000
Emily Foster: Hmm.

01:13:23.000 --> 01:13:35.000
stlic: and now we know you have an event, and a day, and a location, and all that. But, like, if you're not already searching for that, how do we get into that, um, that mindset, or that mind…

01:13:35.000 --> 01:13:39.000
stlic: place for those people who aren't searching for.

01:13:39.000 --> 01:13:51.000
stlic: you know, and is it luxury weddings? Is it, you know, uh… I'm not sure what the… the key terms, or how to… how to navigate that, so…

01:13:50.000 --> 01:14:08.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, and it's not going to be the same for every market, um, but yeah, I would say, like, getting into the head of their pain points and finding keywords related to that in your area. So maybe it is, like, wedding decor in San Antonio, or, like, Austin, um, wedding reception ideas, and, like.

01:14:08.000 --> 01:14:23.000
Emily Foster: having blog content about those, um, will be really good, and then having clear call-to-actions throughout your website that link to frequently asked questions. And in your frequently asked questions, you could have mentions of, like… like, it could be, like, um…

01:14:23.000 --> 01:14:38.000
Emily Foster: if we purchase an ice sculpture, like, what other decor should we consider investing in? And then maybe mention, like, you may also want to consider marquee lights or champagne towers, like, inquire with us, and we're happy to give you referrals for these, or, like, something like that.

01:14:38.000 --> 01:14:55.000
Emily Foster: So it kind of organically gets those keywords on your site without you having like a dedicated page for a service that's not yours. But definitely thinking about like the pain points, like you basically just said that, but then doing the keyword research there and you could use tools like Moz or Ubersuggest.

01:14:55.000 --> 01:15:08.000
Emily Foster: Um, that's a good way to start SEO keyword research. And then also, I'm sure that there's tools in the vault, too, about, like, if you're going through the Meta Ads Accelerator, like, finding the right keywords for your ads campaigns related to those.

01:15:08.000 --> 01:15:11.000
stlic: Cool. Thank you.

01:15:10.000 --> 01:15:16.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, yeah, of course. Feel free to reach out with any other questions related to that. That's a great question. I love, like, when it's.

01:15:13.000 --> 01:15:15.000
Whitney Hodges: That's great.

01:15:16.000 --> 01:15:32.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah, I think that was really good advice, Emily, and I'm not sure on your end, like, on the more organic side, how much you see Pinterest playing into the blogging strategy as well, like, if it makes sense that he's gonna, like, be kind of, like, creating blog posts that are dedicated to these, like, wedding decor trends.

01:15:25.000 --> 01:15:28.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, here.

01:15:32.000 --> 01:15:37.000
Whitney Hodges: To then create some pins that align with that as well, and maybe…

01:15:36.000 --> 01:15:56.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, I think put it everywhere. Yeah, Pinterest is a great way. And then like, that's another great place. I might have mentioned this when we were talking about blog strategy, but having tons of pictures of ice sculptures with these kind of adjacent services, like even if it is like you had a wedding and, you know, a marquee light company has their lights in the background, that's okay to use a picture like that.

01:15:37.000 --> 01:15:38.000
Whitney Hodges: just…

01:15:56.000 --> 01:16:13.000
Emily Foster: Um, but then having the pins do that, and then you can also continue sharing, like, on your stories, and create organic content, like, through Instagram and TikTok and Facebook, and, um, basically, like, promote it that way. And I also, I don't think that you're getting into dangerous territory of, like, false advertising if you have an ad.

01:16:13.000 --> 01:16:28.000
Emily Foster: like, where all of the messaging is focused on ice sculptures, but then you kind of… like, let's say you have, like, a reception table, and there's, like, an ice sculpture, and then a champagne tower next to it. Like, it's not like you're selling the champagne tower, but the overall image of that.

01:16:28.000 --> 01:16:35.000
Emily Foster: ad would give people the idea that, like, okay, this is going to go well with these other trends that I'm interested in.

01:16:31.000 --> 01:16:32.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, just a minute.

01:16:34.000 --> 01:16:48.000
stlic: Yeah, or just like, oh, you're considering marquee lights, also maybe consider an ice sculpture to make the impact you're looking for, where it's like…

01:16:45.000 --> 01:16:47.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, I…

01:16:48.000 --> 01:16:52.000
stlic: If they're searching for a marquee light, you know.

01:16:52.000 --> 01:17:08.000
stlic: it's like, we don't do that, but you can consider this. I mean, I don't think it's, like, ethically wrong or unclear that I also sell marquee lights, but it's just, like, a way for… in my mind, if people are searching for… and I'm…

01:16:56.000 --> 01:16:58.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, I…

01:16:59.000 --> 01:17:01.000
Emily Foster: No.

01:17:08.000 --> 01:17:18.000
stlic: not trying to pick on marquee lighting, it's just something that I've noticed. So, anything like that, like, are you considering XYZ?

01:17:13.000 --> 01:17:15.000
Emily Foster: Yeah.

01:17:18.000 --> 01:17:24.000
stlic: you know, maybe consider ICE as well, or have you thought about this instead, or something?

01:17:21.000 --> 01:17:23.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, I…

01:17:24.000 --> 01:17:43.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, I feel like a great way to think about it, like, a good analogy for this is, like, the way that Oreos basically uses milk to sell their product, but it's, like, just thinking, like, in your ads creative about, like, how can you make, like, the marquee lights or the champagne tower, like, the milk in the background kind of experience of, like, oh, if you're going to have this thing anyway.

01:17:43.000 --> 01:17:50.000
Emily Foster: then you can also buy this other thing that pairs really well with it. So kind of, like, that complimentary advertising.

01:17:51.000 --> 01:18:07.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah. Yeah. Kristi mentioned things that are IG-worthy, eye sculptures, lighting, eye-catching things. So, I mean, there definitely is an area of opportunity with the meta ads. It's just that, you know, that strategy does have to be a little bit more in-depth than, like.

01:17:54.000 --> 01:17:55.000
Emily Foster: Bye-bye.

01:17:58.000 --> 01:17:59.000
Emily Foster: Yeah.

01:18:02.000 --> 01:18:03.000
Emily Foster: Okay.

01:18:07.000 --> 01:18:29.000
Whitney Hodges: somebody who's coming in cold just thinking about an ice sculpture for the first time, they're not gonna be ready to send out fill out that form of your Sean that somebody who's, like, super qualified, like, ready to go would be. Right? So there would have to be those touch points in between of the email marketing or text message marketing, things that are really nurturing them and and kind of.

01:18:29.000 --> 01:18:35.000
Whitney Hodges: warming them up to take them from that awareness stage to actually becoming a lead.

01:18:35.000 --> 01:18:45.000
Emily Foster: Yeah, yeah, and that blog and Pinterest content is a great way to, like, warm up too, like, because you can always, like, have that kind of part of your, like, inquiry or sales funnel too.

01:18:43.000 --> 01:18:44.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, here we go.

01:18:44.000 --> 01:18:45.000
Whitney Hodges: Thank you.

01:18:45.000 --> 01:18:56.000
stlic: Yeah, and Whitney, I know you guys are great about automating, uh, responses and stuff, but, like, that's part of my question, like, I kind of have a philosophy, like.

01:18:56.000 --> 01:19:09.000
stlic: either keep bugging them until they tell me no, or until the date passes that I can execute the event? Um… but also, like, how much time or dedication do you give to just these, like.

01:18:59.000 --> 01:19:00.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

01:19:02.000 --> 01:19:04.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

01:19:09.000 --> 01:19:23.000
stlic: Oh, yeah, that might be neat. I get married in 18 months, like, you know, you have to bring them along and kind of keep, uh, uh, nurturing the idea, but…

01:19:11.000 --> 01:19:13.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

01:19:16.000 --> 01:19:17.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

01:19:23.000 --> 01:19:33.000
stlic: you know, that was a problem that we faced was, like, the admins were spending so much time sending follow-up emails, and, like, uh…

01:19:33.000 --> 01:19:40.000
stlic: Automating felt kind of spammy, and was hard to track responses to, and so we felt like…

01:19:40.000 --> 01:19:57.000
stlic: Man, we're getting hundreds of leads, these warm leads of people that think, Oh, that looks cool, I'm interested. And now we got their name in their email. But now it's like they don't have an event, they don't have a date, they don't have nothing. And we're spending so much time and energy.

01:19:57.000 --> 01:20:13.000
stlic: Trying to keep, hey, you know, let us know. Let us know. Hey, how can we help? Did you know this? Did you know that? And it felt like Man, we're just throwing our resources away right now Because these folks don't have an event or date or.

01:20:13.000 --> 01:20:15.000
Emily Foster: Mm-hmm.

01:20:13.000 --> 01:20:17.000
stlic: any of this. So, what's… do you guys have, like, a…

01:20:17.000 --> 01:20:18.000
Emily Foster: you…

01:20:17.000 --> 01:20:23.000
stlic: Rule of thumb on that, like, yeah, you should send once a week, or once a month.

01:20:23.000 --> 01:20:33.000
stlic: just keep sending them your normal blog email from the MailChimp campaign, and uh… hope for the best, or what… or when do you draw the line at?

01:20:33.000 --> 01:20:39.000
stlic: No, just let that one go because it's taking up too much time.

01:20:40.000 --> 01:20:56.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah, so from like an inquiry standpoint, when inquiries are coming in from ads, usually like the initial nurture sequence can be 30-ish days is kind of what we're seeing as a kind of best practice.

01:20:56.000 --> 01:21:01.000
Whitney Hodges: At this point, but then, I mean, continued follow-ups, like.

01:21:01.000 --> 01:21:17.000
Whitney Hodges: potentially for, like, the next 6 months or so, you know what I mean? Like, somebody who's planning a wedding, right? And depending on if the wedding is actually booked yet, and how far the date is in the future, and, like, just adding on an ice sculpture, that could be an impulse purchase that they could add, I would think.

01:21:17.000 --> 01:21:27.000
Whitney Hodges: pretty close up to the date of the wedding. Um, I do agree that it's way too heavy of a lift to, like, be having your admins manually just, like.

01:21:27.000 --> 01:21:46.000
Whitney Hodges: touching these people over and over again. And oftentimes, also, what we see is that, like, the just checking in, or how are you doing sort of emails don't necessarily, like, get you anywhere, as opposed to, like, a nurture marketing email that's providing more value, that's educating them, that's showing them beautiful pictures, that's maybe linking to blog posts that have been created.

01:21:46.000 --> 01:22:05.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, sort of thing. So, I, yeah, I definitely think the key in kind of mastering a meta ad strategy would be that the email marketing component of it is already kind of put together, that's being done pretty consistently, and in addition to those, like, sequences that, um, people are being pulled into.

01:22:05.000 --> 01:22:16.000
Whitney Hodges: Um, that from there, you know, there is probably at least just a general monthly email that's going out to your full email list, which it sounds like you're kind of already doing already.

01:22:22.000 --> 01:22:23.000
Emily Foster: That's good. Bye.

01:22:22.000 --> 01:22:33.000
Whitney Hodges: So, thank you guys so much, everybody, for being here, um, and spending your morning with us, and thank you, of course, so much to Emily. I think this was super valuable, and…

01:22:30.000 --> 01:22:34.000
Emily Foster: Thank you for having me. I hope it was helpful for you all.

01:22:34.000 --> 01:22:47.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm, it was great. So, um, yeah, we'll send out the replay, we'll make sure everybody has access to these resources, and I think everybody knows where to find me and Emily if you have further questions, or if you need anything.

01:22:47.000 --> 01:22:50.000
Emily Foster: Perfect. Thank you so much, everyone.

01:22:50.000 --> 01:22:53.000
Whitney Hodges: Y'all have a great day. Thanks so much. Bye.

01:22:51.000 --> 01:22:52.000
Emily Foster: Bye!

